  <div class="abstract">
    <p data-label="Summary">The Gradient block models the smooth sky that light pollution and the Moon add to the
    stack, and the vignetting of the sky background, and removes it from what every later block reads. It offers three models: a
    polynomial surface fitted on blocks of sky, a thin-plate spline through samples of sky, and an automatic surface
    fitted on every pixel that does not look like an object. Only the variation of the sky across the field is
    removed, not its level. An H-alpha survey of the field, when there is one, keeps the samples away from the
    nebulosity.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="chain" aria-label="Position in the graph">
    <li><span>Selection</span></li><li><span>Stacking</span></li><li><span>Astrometry</span></li>
    <li class="here"><span>Gradient</span></li><li><span>Deconvolution</span></li><li><span>Denoise</span></li>
  </ol>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>What goes in, what comes out</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Why on the stack.</b> The block works on the linear stack, in the camera’s colours, before
    the colour calibration. The later blocks (denoising, colour, star removal, the output) then see a flat sky.</aside>
    <p>The block reads the stack and the map of how many frames cover each pixel. Pixels that fewer than 60 % of the
    typical number of frames cover, and that reach the border of the stack, are left out; so are the areas
    excluded in the editor.</p>
    <p>It writes the sky’s variation for each of the three colours, with a median of 0 over the covered field, and the
    level of the sky it leaves in place. Later blocks subtract the variation from the stack and the half-stacks A
    and B alike (section 5 of the <a href="/blocks/stacking/#s5">Stacking</a> page), so both share the same sky. The
    sky’s level is kept on purpose: the noise models of the later blocks rely on it.</p>
    <p>FLOAT also reports how large the gradient was: the spread of the removed surface across the field (from its 1st
    to its 99th percentile), as a percentage of the sky’s level. The block’s preview shows the stack with the gradient removed.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>Polynomial</h2>
    <p>A polynomial of the pixel position, fitted for each colour on samples of sky.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> With no sample placed, the field
    is cut into blocks; the side of the grid is the “Samples per side” setting, and each cell of the grid holds about
    4 × 4 blocks. The median of a block is one sample.</p>
    <p>The samples of sky are found on the luminance, over the whole field, in a loop. The surface is fitted on the
    samples; the samples that lie more than 2.5 sigmas above it are left out; the surface is fitted again, until the
    set no longer changes. The sigma is the scatter between neighbouring samples, so it measures the noise and the
    fine structure of the sky, not the objects, which change slowly from one block to the next. Samples below the
    surface are all kept: the sky is there.</p>
    <aside class="note"><b>A large object.</b> An object that fills most of the field, a large galaxy for example, is
    left out whole and the surface passes under it.</aside>
    <p>Each colour is then fitted on those samples, leaving out, in turn, the ones farther than 2.5 sigmas from its own
    surface. A block counts as a sample only if the stack covers it at 99 % or more. Under 10 such blocks, or with
    fewer than three times as many samples as the surface has terms, the block stops and says so: lower the degree or
    the number of samples.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>RBF</h2>
    <p>A thin-plate spline (a radial basis function) through the samples of
    sky.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> The surface passes near every sample,
    so it can follow a gradient of any shape; it also depends on where the samples are. The “Surface smoothing”
    setting sets how near: at 0 the surface passes through every sample, higher it passes near them and is
    smoother.</p>
    <p>With no sample placed, the samples are a regular grid with “Samples per side” points along the shorter side of
    the image, a square box around each. The samples kept are the ones not above a degree-2 surface through them
    by more than 2.5 sigmas, found as in section 2. The coordinates and the values of each colour are rescaled before the fit, so that the
    smoothing means the same on every image.</p>
    <p>The samples of the polynomial and of the RBF can also be placed by hand in the editor, and areas excluded
    there, as ellipses or painted strokes. Placed samples are always kept as placed.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>Automatic</h2>
    <p>A model with no samples, after the automatic method of background extraction of
    Siril.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> It works on each colour apart, on a copy of the stack reduced 4 times by block means
    (“Working reduction”), with the image scaled to 0 to 1 between its darkest and its brightest pixels (0.01th and
    99.99th percentiles of the covered field, with 20 % of headroom above). The background is first fitted on every
    pixel; then, for at most 20 rounds, until fewer than 1 pixel in 10,000 changes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the residual is the image minus the background; a pixel is kept when its residual lies between the median
      minus 4 sigmas and the median plus 2 sigmas, sigma being 1.4826 times the median absolute deviation of the
      residual over the whole covered field. Bright structures go first;</li>
      <li>with “Structure protection”, a smooth mask grown around the pixels whose residual exceeds the “Protection
      threshold” (0.05, on the 0 to 1 image) is left out too. “Protection growth” sets how far the mask grows. With
      “Noise protection” other than 0, the threshold is that many times the noise of the sky instead;</li>
      <li>never fewer than 2 % of the pixels are kept;</li>
      <li>the background is fitted again on the kept pixels.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The surface is built by filling the rejected areas from the kept pixels with repeated low-pass filters (three
    running box blurs that match a Gaussian<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>), then filtering once more. The radius of the filter is “Model scale”
    percent of the shorter side of the reduced image, 5 by default. A last low-pass, of “Model smoothness” times that
    radius, smooths the model; it is brought back to full size by bilinear interpolation. At the edges of the image,
    each blur continues the image along its slope, so that the surface does not sag towards the border.</p>
    <p>With “Plane first”, a stiff polynomial (a plane by default) is fitted and removed first, and the flexible model
    works on what it leaves. This helps on gradients as strong as the objects themselves: a bright urban sky, a strong
    Moon gradient.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>The H-alpha survey as a guide</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Never during a run.</b> A survey is fetched once by the Get survey button of the Gradient
    editor, or by the download for offline use in the settings, and kept on disk. A run only reads it.</aside>
    <p>Where an H-alpha survey covers the field, FLOAT reprojects it onto the stack and uses it to choose
    the samples: with “Away from emission” on, the samples the block chooses (none placed) never sit where the survey
    shows nebulosity. The faint extensions of an emission nebula are then not taken for sky. The survey only says where
    not to sample; the sky model never reads its values.</p>
    <p>The survey is scaled so that its sky is 0 and its bright emission 1 (1st and 99.5th percentiles of the field). A
    sample sits on emission when the median of that level in its box is above 0.02. The survey is used only if its
    range is at least 12 times its own noise; one that shows only noise leaves every sample. It is not used either
    when it would leave less than 5 % of the covered field for the samples, or too little sky for the fit: the block
    then chooses its samples on the image alone, and says so in the log. The automatic model takes no samples, so the
    survey does not act on it.</p>
    <p>With the survey setting on “auto”, FLOAT uses NSNS when it covers at least 95 % of the field, otherwise the
    all-sky Finkbeiner map.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a> The setting can
    also name one of them, or “none”. A field that was not identified by the <a href="/blocks/astrometry/">Astrometry</a> block has no survey.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>The settings of the block. The survey settings belong to the H-alpha reference step that feeds it.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Method</th><td>Polynomial</td><td>Polynomial, RBF, Automatic</td><td>How the sky gradient is modelled (sections 2 to 4).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Surface degree</th><td class="n">2</td><td class="n">1 – 5</td><td>Degree of the polynomial. 3 or 4 for wide fields with complex gradients; a high degree on a field filled with nebula can take a part of it for sky.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Samples per side</th><td class="n">16</td><td class="n">4 – 64</td><td>With no sample placed. Polynomial: the field is cut into 4 times this many blocks per side. RBF: this many samples along the shorter side. More, smaller blocks find sky in a crowded field; fewer, larger ones average more noise.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Object rejection</th><td class="n">2.5 σ</td><td class="n">1 – 10</td><td>Samples farther from the surface than this are taken as objects and left out, in turn.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Surface smoothing</th><td class="n">0.5</td><td class="n">0 – 1</td><td>RBF: 0, the surface passes through every sample; higher, it passes near them, smoother.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Away from emission</th><td>on</td><td>on, off</td><td>The samples the block chooses stay off the nebulosity the H-alpha survey shows. Samples placed by hand are always kept.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Model scale</th><td class="n">5</td><td class="n">1 – 10</td><td>Automatic: the scale below which variations are taken as objects rather than gradient. Higher keeps only the large gradients; lower follows more local ones and may take large faint objects for sky.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Model smoothness</th><td class="n">1</td><td class="n">0 – 5</td><td>Automatic: extra smoothing of the final model (0: none).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Structure protection</th><td>on</td><td>on, off</td><td>Automatic: masks the extended bright structures so that the model does not take them in.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Protection threshold</th><td class="n">0.05</td><td class="n">0 – 1</td><td>Automatic: brightness above the model, on the image scaled to 0 to 1, at which a pixel is taken as a structure. Lower protects more.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Protection growth</th><td class="n">0.5</td><td class="n">0 – 1</td><td>Automatic: how far the protection mask grows around the structures.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Noise protection</th><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">0 – 50</td><td>Automatic: when not 0, the protection threshold is this many times the noise of the sky, which suits a faint nebula in a field where bright stars set the range.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Plane first</th><td>off</td><td>on, off</td><td>Automatic: a stiff polynomial is fitted and removed before the flexible model.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Plane-first degree</th><td class="n">1</td><td class="n">1 – 6</td><td>Degree of that polynomial (1: a plane).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Working reduction</th><td class="n">4 ×</td><td class="n">1, 2, 4, 8</td><td>Automatic: the model is computed on a copy reduced this many times: faster, coarser. The scale of the background itself does not change.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sample points</th><td>none</td><td>placed in the editor</td><td>The samples placed on the image. Empty: the block’s own grid.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Excluded ellipses, Excluded strokes</th><td>none</td><td>drawn in the editor</td><td>Areas that never enter any of the three models.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">H-alpha survey</th><td>auto</td><td>auto, nsns, finkbeiner, none</td><td>Which survey guides the samples (section 5).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">NSNS coverage</th><td class="n">0.95</td><td class="n">0 – 1</td><td>Below this fraction of the field covered by NSNS, “auto” switches to Finkbeiner.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s7"><span class="secnum">7</span>Limits, and what was tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The polynomial and the automatic model trade against each other.</strong> The polynomial keeps faint,
      very wide structures best but follows only gradients of its own shape. The automatic model removes complex
      gradients best, and with them part of the faintest wide structures.</li>
      <li><strong>A model that looks only at the image cannot tell a very wide faint cloud from a gradient.</strong>
      A lower model scale, a high degree and Plane first each can take part of a large faint or bright object
      for sky.</li>
      <li><strong>The RBF depends on where its samples are.</strong> It is best with samples placed by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Frames stacked without alignment.</strong> When too little of the stack is covered by all the frames,
      the block cannot model the sky and asks for <a href="/blocks/registration/">Registration</a> to be placed before <a href="/blocks/stacking/">Stacking</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The survey is a guide, not a reference.</strong> It does not exist for every field, and it is not used
      when the field was not identified. A method that fitted the survey as part of the true sky, with a gain, was
      tried and removed: it did not make the surface more flexible, and on a field with little H-alpha and much dust the
      fitted gain ran away and attributed to the survey light that was not its own.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

